Fixed Size Chunking: This is the simplest and most commonly used approach.
In fixed-size chunking, the document is split into chunks containing a fixed number of tokens or characters.
Each chunk is stored separately in the vector database.
Easy to implement, Fast preprocessing, Works well for quick prototypes but
Sentences or paragraphs may get cut in the middle
Important context can be lost across chunks

Sentence or Paragraph Chunking: Instead of splitting by token length, this strategy splits text using natural language boundaries -
Periods, Commas, Question marks, Paragraph breaks.
This approach keeps complete ideas together, making it easier for the LLM to understand the context.
Best for - Articles, Blogs, FAQs, Documentation but
Paragraphs can sometimes become too long or inconsistent, which can affect retrieval.

Recursive Chunking: It attempts to preserve document structure.
Instead of splitting everything the same way, it tries multiple levels.
The algorithm starts with large blocks and keeps splitting until the chunk fits the desired token size.
Maintains document structure, Produces more meaningful chunks

Semantic Chunking:
It is considered one of the most intelligent chunking strategies.
Its covered in the video, and advantages are Higher retrieval accuracy, Better contextual understanding. But
More computationally expensive, Requires additional embedding calculations

Page-Level Chunking: In some documents, each page already represents a logical unit of information. Like in Resumes, Invoices, Research reports, financial statements.
In such cases, each page becomes a chunk.
This approach preserves document structure and layout.
However, it may fail if important information spans multiple pages.

Code-Aware Chunking:
When building RAG systems for software documentation or codebases, traditional text chunking does not work well.
Instead, chunking should follow programming structures such as: Functions, Classes, Modules, Scopes.
Each logical unit becomes a separate chunk.
This significantly improves retrieval accuracy for developer tools.

