1: Don't treat every PDF the same

A common mistake is extracting everything as plain text.

But PDFs can contain:

📄 Text documents

📊 Tables

📈 Charts

🖼️ Scanned images

Each requires a different extraction strategy.

Step 2: Parse documents intelligently

For text:

Extract structured text
Preserve headings and sections

For tables:

Extract rows and columns separately
Maintain relationships between cells

For scanned PDFs:

Apply OCR

For charts:

Use multimodal models to generate chart descriptions

Example:

Instead of storing:

"Revenue chart"

Store:

"Revenue increased from $2M in 2022 to $5M in 2024."

Now the LLM can reason over it.

Step 3: Create semantic chunks

❌ Bad chunking

Split every 500 characters.

✅ Good chunking

Chunk by:

Headings
Sections
Table boundaries
Semantic meaning

This dramatically improves retrieval quality.

Step 4: Generate embeddings

Convert chunks into vectors and store them in a vector database.

Examples:

Pinecone
Weaviate
Milvus

Now similar questions can retrieve similar content.

Step 5: Hybrid Retrieval

Don't rely only on vector search.

Use:

🔹 Keyword Search (BM25)

🔹 Vector Search

🔹 Metadata Filtering

This helps when users search for:

Invoice numbers
Product IDs
Exact dates
Names
Step 6: Add Reranking

Retriever ≠ Best Result

Retrieve Top 20

Rerank Top 20

Send Top 5 to the LLM

This often improves answer quality more than switching to a larger model.

Step 7: Use a Multimodal RAG Pipeline

Since PDFs contain charts and images:

Store:

Text + Tables + OCR + Image Descriptions

Not just text.

This is where most RAG systems fail.

Step 8: Add Citations

Every answer should include:

. Source PDF

. Page Number

.Retrieved Chunk

Users trust answers they can verify.

